      "title": "Lesson 33: AYMĀN – OATHS",
      "overview": "This lesson is regarding the Islamic practice of ritual sacrifice (Udḥiyah or Qurbānī) and its spiritual, ethical, and legal dimensions within Islamic law. It explains that the act of sacrifice is fundamentally an expression of devotion, submission, and sincerity toward Allah, rather than a cultural custom or financial transaction. The lesson highlights that the meat of the sacrificed animal may be consumed, stored, or distributed in charity, while emphasizing that selling any part of the animal—such as its hide—is generally discouraged unless it is exchanged for a durable household item without the intention of profit. Particular stress is placed on maintaining purity of intention, clarifying that any monetary gain derived from the sacrifice must be given in charity to preserve the integrity of the act of worship.\n\nThis lesson is also regarding the concept of oaths (aymān) in Islamic jurisprudence and the legal consequences attached to them. It outlines the three primary categories of oaths: false oaths concerning past events, which are sinful and require repentance; enacted oaths related to future actions, which necessitate expiation if broken; and unintentional oaths made under mistaken belief, which do not carry legal accountability. The lesson underscores the gravity of invoking Allah’s name, explaining that oaths are binding even if made in jest or under certain forms of pressure, provided the legal conditions are fulfilled. Overall, the discussion emphasizes the importance of understanding both ritual sacrifice and oaths as serious acts connected to faith, responsibility, and moral accountability in Islamic law.",
