      "title": "Lesson 34: AYMĀN – OATHS",
      "overview": "In this lesson, we examined the jurisprudential and theological principles governing oath-taking (al-yamīn) in Islamic law, with particular focus on the requirement that a valid oath must be made by invoking the name of Allah or one of His recognized attributes. We clarified that an oath is only legally binding when it is sworn by Allah Himself—either through His personal name “Allah” or through one of His divine names such as Ar-Raḥmān or Ar-Raḥīm. The lesson explored the distinction between Allah’s unique proper name and His other names, which function as titles derived from His eternal attributes. We discussed the theological principle that attributes are foundational, while names are derived from those attributes—for example, Allah is called Al-Qadīr because of His attribute of qudrah (power). This framework helps explain why invoking certain names constitutes a valid oath while other expressions may not.\n\nWe also analyzed the conditions that determine whether an oath is legally recognized, emphasizing the role of custom (ʿurf) and intention (niyyah). The lesson distinguished between essential attributes, such as knowledge and power, and attributes of action, such as wrath and mercy, explaining that not all attributes are commonly used in oath-taking. An oath sworn by an attribute of action is only considered valid if it is customarily recognized as such within Islamic practice. Finally, we concluded by discussing the legal and spiritual consequences of breaking a valid oath, including the obligation of expiation (kaffārah) and the removal of sin through fulfilling the prescribed requirements. This comprehensive discussion demonstrated the close relationship between theology, language, intention, and legal rulings in the Islamic tradition.",
