      "portalId": "c2a7aeac-592a-4c9e-9bab-599468247ce9",
      "title": "Lesson 35: AYMĀN – OATHS. Expiation for the Breach of Oath",
      "overview": "In this lesson, we discuss the nuances of taking oaths in Islam, focusing on the attributes of Allah that may be used when making an oath. We explain the distinction between attributes of essence and attributes of action, emphasizing that an oath is valid if it is customarily taken by an attribute, regardless of which category it belongs to. We highlight the opinions of various Islamic scholars, especially the majority view which combines both categories into one and permits oaths by any attribute of Allah as long as it is customarily used. We also warn against swearing by anything other than Allah, His names, or His attributes, as this is considered haram and may lead to disbelief (kufr).\n\nWe further explore the concept of expiation (kafara) for breaking oaths, outlining the available options such as feeding or clothing the poor, freeing a slave, or fasting if one is unable to afford the other options. We clarify that expiation only becomes necessary after an oath has been broken and cannot be performed beforehand. In addition, we address the subject of vows (nathr) and the conditions that determine when they must be fulfilled, distinguishing between unconditional and conditional vows. We also provide guidance on dealing with vows that are linked to undesirable actions, explaining that they may be treated like oaths with the option of expiation. This lesson aims to clarify the legal and spiritual aspects of oaths and vows in Islamic practice.",
